patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ count: 0 }`.

Helpers: `whole(x) = Number.isFinite(x) ? Math.floor(x + 1e-6) : 0` (the epsilon keeps 2.9999999999999996 from float math at 3) and `wrap(n, m) = ((n % m) + m) % m`.

Each frame:
```
const max = whole(ctx.input("maximumCount"));
let n = state.count;
if (ctx.pulsed("jump")) {
  const target = whole(ctx.input("jumpToNumber"));
  n = max > 0 && (target < 0 || target >= max) ? 0 : target;
} else {
  n += (ctx.pulsed("increase") ? 1 : 0) - (ctx.pulsed("decrease") ? 1 : 0);
}
if (max > 0) n = wrap(n, max);
state.count = n;
ctx.output("count", n);
```

- Pulses: `increase`, `decrease`, and `jump` are pulses (`ctx.pulsed`: an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). Pulses on consecutive frames each count, so a pulse into Increase on 3 back-to-back frames adds 3.
- Precedence (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.2): `jump` > (`increase` − `decrease`). When Jump fires, Increase and Decrease are ignored that frame. Increase and Decrease in the same frame cancel.
- Wrapping (CONVENTIONS.md §18.5.2): `maximumCount ≤ 0` means unconstrained, so any integer including negatives. Otherwise the count stays within `[0, maximumCount − 1]`: increasing from the top goes to 0, decreasing from 0 goes to `maximumCount − 1`. A jump target outside that range sets 0 (the starting value), not a wrapped value.
- Retargeting: a new `maximumCount` re-wraps the stored count on that frame even without a pulse (count 4 with a new maximum of 3 becomes 1). Raising the maximum again keeps the wrapped count. Changing `jumpToNumber` does nothing until Jump fires.
- Frame 0: the count starts at 0. A pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0.
- Loops: per-index state. New indices start at 0; removed indices drop their state. A scalar pulse broadcasts, so every index steps together.
- Edge cases: a non-finite `jumpToNumber` or `maximumCount` counts as 0. Fractional inputs round down after the epsilon (2.5 → 2, −1.5 → −2). The output is always a finite integer.
- Restart and dispose: restart resets every index to 0. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: same input labels; Origami's output is unnamed. Origami documents wrap-around and the out-of-range jump reset but not same-frame precedence or fractional inputs. The legacy Counter macro stored an unwrapped count and wrapped only its output; Sonobe stores the wrapped count so the visible number is the state.

State per loop index: `{ previous: false, seeded: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const on = ctx.input<boolean>("on");
if (state.seeded) {
  if (on && !state.previous) ctx.pulse("turnedOn");
  if (!on && state.previous) ctx.pulse("turnedOff");
}
state.previous = on;
state.seeded = true;
```

- Pulses: both outputs use `ctx.pulse` and last exactly one frame. At most one of them fires per frame per index. A state that alternates every frame fires Turned On and Turned Off on alternating frames.
- Input: `on` is a boolean state port, not a pulse port. Numbers coerce with `> 0`. An upstream pulse reads as `true` for one frame, so Turned On fires on that frame and Turned Off on the next. Pulses on back-to-back frames read as one longer `true`, so Turned On fires once.
- Frame 0 (gap-fill R1): the first evaluation of each index only records the value. A state that starts on doesn't fire Turned On.
- Loops: per-index history. A loop on On outputs loops of pulses. New indices seed on their first evaluation without firing; removed indices drop their state.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears history, so the first frame after a restart seeds again without firing. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami labels the input "On / Off". Origami inputs hold defaults on frame 0, so a state that starts on can fire on frame 1; Sonobe evaluates authored values on frame 0 and never fires on a first evaluation.

State per loop index: `{ previous: undefined, variant: undefined, seeded: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const value = ctx.input("value");
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (!state.seeded || state.variant !== variant) {
  state.previous = value;
  state.variant = variant;
  state.seeded = true;
} else if (!same(value, state.previous)) {
  ctx.pulse("changed");
  state.previous = value;
}
```

`same(a, b)` by variant:
- number and index: `a === b` (exact; `0 === -0`).
- boolean, enum, and text: strict equality. Text is case-sensitive, with no trimming or Unicode normalization.
- color: equal when r, g, b, and a each round to the same 8-bit value (`Math.round(c * 255)`), the precision documents store colors with.
- point, point3d, point4d, size, anchor: every component `===`.
- json: deep structural equality. Same kind (null, boolean, number, string, array, object); arrays match element by element in order; objects have the same key set with equal values, in any key order.

Keep explicit history as above; don't substitute `ctx.changed("value")` unless it matches these equality and first-frame rules.

- Pulses: `changed` lasts exactly one frame per `ctx.pulse`. A value that changes on consecutive frames fires on each of those frames (ARCHITECTURE.md §4).
- Frame 0 (gap-fill R1): the first evaluation of each index seeds `previous` and never fires.
- Loops: per-index history. A loop on Value outputs a loop of pulses, firing only at the indices that changed. When the loop grows, new indices seed without firing; when it shrinks, removed indices drop their history.
- Retargeting: changing the patch's type (`typeParam`) re-seeds every index without firing.
- Edge cases: an unconnected Value never changes, so it never fires. Coercion happens at the port before comparing: a number wired into a text-typed patch compares as formatted text.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears history, so the first frame after a restart seeds again without firing. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's output is unnamed; Sonobe calls it Changed. Origami fixed a false pulse on first evaluation in v188; Sonobe never fires on a first evaluation.

State per loop index: `{ variant: undefined, held: undefined }`.

Each frame:
```
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (state.variant !== variant) {
  state.variant = variant;
  state.held = zeroValue(variant);
}
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.held = zeroValue(variant);
if (ctx.input<boolean>("sample")) state.held = ctx.input("value");
ctx.output("output", state.held);
```

- Zero values (CONVENTIONS.md §8): number and index `0`; boolean `false`; text `""`; color `#00000000`; vectors all zeros; enum the first option; json, image, video, sound, gradient, shape, layerEffect, and layer `null`.
- Pulses: `reset` is a pulse input (`ctx.pulsed`). `sample` is a boolean state port that samples on every frame it's true: a wired pulse samples exactly one frame, and a held state tracks continuously. Numbers coerce with `> 0`.
- Precedence: reset runs first, then sample, so a same-frame sample wins and Output shows the new value.
- Timing: a sampled value appears on the same frame, with no one-frame delay.
- Frame 0: `held` starts at the zero value. If `sample` is true on frame 0, frame 0's value is captured.
- Loops: per-index storage. A loop on Value or Sample stores one value per index, such as one start position per looped card. When Sample is a scalar `true`, every index captures its own item. New indices start empty; removed indices drop their state.
- Retargeting: changing the patch's type resets every index to the new type's zero value. Value changes never reach Output while Sample is false.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears every index to the zero value. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: same ports. Origami doesn't document a same-frame Reset and Sample; Sonobe lets the sample win.
